C = [
("h1", "10. Who Do Men Say? The Crowds, Herod and the Cross"),
("p", "Claim: the Gospels keep three lists of what people said about Jesus, and in every list John and Elias appear as two separate persons. The lists come after John’s death. At the cross the bystanders think the Lord calls for Elias and wait to see whether he will come. Between those two scenes the disciples ask the Lord to bring down fire as Elias did, and he rebukes them. The chapter reads these passages as written, in order. It does not use them to settle the question; it records what the texts show about how the people thought of Elias."),
("sub", "10.1 Herod and the three lists"),
("p", "Herod the tetrarch hears of the Lord’s fame and says, “This is John the Baptist; he is risen from the dead; and therefore mighty works do shew forth themselves in him” (Matthew 14:1, 2). Mark records the same, and adds what others said: “Others said, That it is Elias. And others said, That it is a prophet, or as one of the prophets” (Mark 6:14, 15). Luke has it the same way: Herod “was perplexed, because that it was said of some, that John was risen from the dead; and of some, that Elias had appeared; and of others, that one of the old prophets was risen again” (Luke 9:7, 8). Matthew has already told of John’s end: “And he sent, and beheaded John in the prison” (Matthew 14:10). Herod’s own verdict is, in Mark, “It is John, whom I beheaded: he is risen from the dead” (Mark 6:16)."),
("p", "Three positions stand side by side in the lists: John risen, Elias appeared, and one of the prophets. Elias is not John in this list. The people who say “Elias” are not the people who say “John,” and Herod’s group, who say John, are not the same as those who say Elias. The lists come after John’s death, and the opinion that Elias had “appeared” stands as a separate opinion even though the one the Lord had called Elias in Matthew 11:14 had already been killed."),
("sub", "10.2 Caesarea Philippi"),
("p", "The Lord asks his disciples what men say. “Whom do men say that I the Son of man am? And they said, Some say that thou art John the Baptist: some, Elias; and others, Jeremias, or one of the prophets” (Matthew 16:13, 14). Mark: “John the Baptist: but some say, Elias; and others, One of the prophets” (Mark 8:28). Luke: “John the Baptist; but some say, Elias; and others say, that one of the old prophets is risen again” (Luke 9:19). The disciples report the list, and the Lord goes on to ask what they say. Peter answers, “The Christ of God” (Luke 9:20)."),
("p", "The wording is steady across the three accounts: John, Elias, a prophet. The disciples give the people’s list without comment. The volume notes only that the list names Elias as a separate figure from John a second time, in a scene the Gospels place after John’s death, and that the Lord does not correct the list in the passage."),
("sub", "10.3 The prophet and the Prophet"),
("p", "Two other titles appear in John. After the feeding of the multitude, “those men, when they had seen the miracle that Jesus did, said, This is of a truth that prophet that should come into the world” (John 6:14). At the feast, “Many of the people therefore, when they heard this saying, said, Of a truth this is the Prophet” (John 7:40). “That prophet” is the figure the deputation of John 1:21 asked John about, and he said, No. The volume names the figure and does not take up whom the title names. For the question of Elias, the point is that the people of the Gospels kept a list of expected figures, and Elias was on it."),
("sub", "10.4 At the cross"),
("p", "When the Lord cries from the cross, “Eli, Eli, lama sabachthani?” the bystanders mishear: “Some of them that stood there, when they heard that, said, This man calleth for Elias” (Matthew 27:46, 47). Mark has it, “Behold, he calleth Elias” (Mark 15:35). Then one runs with a sponge, and “the rest said, Let be, let us see whether Elias will come to save him” (Matthew 27:49). Mark’s form is “let us see whether Elias will come to take him down” (Mark 15:36)."),
("p", "The bystanders’ words are the last mention of Elias in the Gospels. They show that a crowd at the cross thought of Elias as a figure who might come to a man in distress, to save him, and who might be called. The text records their expectation and records that no one came. The volume does not interpret the crowd's belief. It reports that a belief in Elias’s coming was present at the cross, in the days after John’s death, in the mouths of those who stood by."),
("sub", "10.5 The fire refused"),
("p", "The last passage is the Samaritan village. “And it came to pass, when the time was come that he should be received up, he stedfastly set his face to go to Jerusalem, and sent messengers before his face: and they went, and entered into a village of the Samaritans, to make ready for him. And they did not receive him, because his face was as though he would go to Jerusalem” (Luke 9:51–53). Then: “And when his disciples James and John saw this, they said, Lord, wilt thou that we command fire to come down from heaven, and consume them, even as Elias did?” (Luke 9:54)."),
("p", "The disciples name Elias as a precedent, and the precedent is 2 Kings 1. The Lord “turned, and rebuked them, and said, Ye know not what manner of spirit ye are of. For the Son of man is not come to destroy men’s lives, but to save them” (Luke 9:55, 56). The answer does not deny that Elias called fire. It says that the disciples do not know what manner of spirit they are of, and that the Son of man has come to save. The passage shows what the disciples drew from the example of Elias and what the Lord refused. It does not identify anyone as Elias, and it does not discuss Malachi."),
("ctbl", "Who the people and the disciples said, and when.", [
    ("Passage", "Who is named", "After John’s death?"),
    ("Matthew 14:1–2; Mark 6:14–16; Luke 9:7–9", "Herod: John risen; others: Elias, a prophet", "Yes; Herod beheaded John"),
    ("Matthew 16:13–14; Mark 8:27–28; Luke 9:18–20", "John the Baptist; Elias; Jeremias or a prophet", "Yes"),
    ("John 6:14; 7:40", "That prophet; the Prophet", "Not stated"),
    ("Matthew 27:46–49; Mark 15:34–36", "The bystanders think he calls Elias and wait to see whether Elias will come", "Yes"),
    ("Luke 9:51–56", "The disciples ask for fire “even as Elias did”; the Lord rebukes them", "Not stated"),
  ], [0.34, 0.42, 0.24]),
("sub", "10.6 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "In every list of what the people said, John and Elias are two persons, and the lists come after John was beheaded.",
    "Herod, the crowds and the disciples’ report all keep “Elias” as a separate opinion from “John the Baptist.”",
    "The bystanders at the cross thought the Lord called Elias and waited to see whether Elias would come to save him.",
    "James and John name Elias as a precedent for calling fire, and the Lord refuses the fire with the words “Ye know not what manner of spirit ye are of.”",
    "None of these passages says who Elias is; they show that expectation of Elias’s coming was alive in the people after John’s death.",
  ]),
]
